# LLM-Assisted Code Generation

## How LLMs Generate Code

* From Autocomplete to Code Generation

- @Motivation@: modern coding assistants draft functions, tests, and entire files
  from a natural-language prompt
  - Not by following hardcoded rules, but by repeatedly predicting the next
    token of code
- @Question@: how does one mechanism, "predict the next token", produce code
  that compiles and runs?
- @Overview@: this lesson covers
  1. How an LLM generates code, token by token
  2. How the prompt and context shape what gets generated
  3. Where code generation breaks down

* Autoregressive Token Prediction

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: an **autoregressive language model** generates a sequence by
  repeatedly predicting the next _token_ conditioned on all previous tokens
  $$\Pr(x_1, ..., x_n) = \prod_{t=1}^n \Pr(x_t | x_1, ..., x_{t-1})$$

// From book Alammar et al., "Hands-On Large Language Models" (2024)
- @Remark@: the same autoregressive mechanism that generates natural-language
  text also generates code, since LLM is trained on code tokenized as text
  [@alammar2024hands]
  - For code, a _token_ is a sub-word unit of code text (e.g., `def`, `return`,
    `:`), not a full line or statement
  - The model never "plans" the whole function at once: it commits to one token,
    then re-reads everything so far to pick the next one
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz[width=50%]
digraph TokenStrip {
    // orange: prefix tokens | blue: token to predict
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    nodesep=0.1;
    ranksep=0.2;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Courier",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.1,0.06", height=0.36, width=1.5,
          fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6];

    T1 [label="def"];
    T2 [label="parity"];
    T3 [label="("];
    T4 [label="n"];
    T5 [label="):"];
    Next [label="next token?", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11,
          fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    T1 -> T2 -> T3 -> T4 -> T5 -> Next;
}
```
label=fig:token-strip
caption=An illustrative split of the prompt into tokens (orange), with the next token (blue) still to be predicted: not real tokenizer output.
::::
:::

* One Token at a Time

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Algorithm@:
  1. Feed the prompt tokens into the model
  2. Model outputs a probability distribution over the next token
  3. Pick a token (greedy, or sampled) [@holtzman2020curious]
  4. Append it, feed the whole sequence back in
  5. Repeat until a stop token or length limit
- @Example@: the prompt `def parity(n):` steers the distribution toward
  `return n % 2 == 0`, one token at a time, because that continuation was
  common after similar signatures in training
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz[width=75%]
digraph TokenLoop {
  rankdir=TB;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11, penwidth=1.5];
  Prompt [label="Prompt tokens", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
  Model  [label="Model", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  Dist   [label="Next-token\ndistribution", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
  Tok    [label="Sampled token", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
  Prompt -> Model -> Dist -> Tok;
  Tok -> Model [label="  append,\l  repeat\l", style=dashed, constraint=false];
}
```
label=fig:token-loop
caption=The generation loop: prompt tokens (orange) go to the model (teal), which outputs a next-token distribution (light blue) and a sampled token (blue) that is appended and fed back.
::::
:::

* Training Data: Public Repos and Documentation

- @Components@: the **training corpus for a code-generation model** includes:
  - _Public repositories_: source files scraped from open-source hosting
  - _Documentation_: READMEs, API docs, and tutorial-style text
  - _Natural-language text_: general text that teaches the model language and
    reasoning, not just syntax

// From paper Huynh et al., "Large Language Models for Code Generation: A
// Comprehensive Survey" (2025)
- @Remark@: code-generation models are typically pretrained or fine-tuned on
  a mix of code and natural language, not code text alone, since:
  - A prompt is natural language; and
  - The desired output is code

* Training Data: Filtering and Synthetic Code

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
// From paper Lozhkov et al., "StarCoder 2 and The Stack v2" (2024)
- @Example@: **StarCoder 2** trains on a large multi-language corpus (The Stack
  v2), with license filtering, deduplication, and quality filtering applied
  before training [@lozhkov2024starcoder]

// From paper Rozière et al., "Code Llama: Open Foundation Models for Code" (2023)
- @Example@: **Code Llama** fine-tunes a general language model on a corpus that
  is roughly 85% code [@roziere2023code]
  - A code-heavy fine-tuning stage can specialize a general model for code

- @Definition@: **synthetic code** is code generated by a model or
  programmatically and added back into training data
  - _Goal_: cover patterns that are rare or absent in scraped repositories
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz
digraph FilteringPipeline {
    // orange: source | teal: filter step | blue: result
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.35;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.16,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Repos [label="Scraped\nrepositories"];
    Synth [label="Synthetic code"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    License [label="License filter"];
    Dedup   [label="Deduplication"];
    Quality [label="Quality filter"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Corpus [label="Training corpus"];

    Repos -> License -> Dedup -> Quality -> Corpus;
    Synth -> Corpus [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", label="  Added back"];
    { rank=same; Quality; Synth; }
}
```
label=fig:filtering-pipeline
caption=Scraped repositories (orange) pass license, deduplication, and quality filters (teal) into the training corpus (blue), which synthetic code (orange) also feeds.
::::
:::

* Case Study: Codex and HumanEval

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// From paper Chen et al., "Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on
// Code" (2021)
- @Example@: **Codex** is a GPT model fine-tuned on public source code,
  introduced together with **HumanEval**, a benchmark of hand-written
  programming problems checked by unit tests [@chen2021evaluating]
  - Codex is the model that originally powered GitHub Copilot
    [@openai2021codex], establishing next-token prediction as a viable basis
    for a production coding assistant

- @Fact@:
  - Codex solved a majority of HumanEval problems under repeated sampling
    [@chen2021evaluating]
  - Despite being trained purely to predict the next token, with no explicit
    notion of "correctness" during training
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz[width=85%]
digraph SampleAndTest {
    // orange: input | teal: model | blue: output | green: test execution
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.15;
    ranksep=0.2;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.05", height=0.34];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Prob [label="Problem:\nsignature +\ndocstring"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Codex [label="Codex"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Samples [label="k sampled\nsolutions"];
    Result  [label="Pass / fail"];

    node [fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Tests [label="Run unit tests"];

    Prob -> Codex -> Samples -> Tests -> Result;
    Result -> Codex [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C",
                     label="  Repeat\l  k times\l", constraint=false];

    subgraph cluster_legend {
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#FBFBFD"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=10;
        node [fontsize=9, height=0.26, width=0.5, margin="0.08,0.04",
              penwidth=1.2];
        L1 [label="Input", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        L2 [label="Model", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
        L3 [label="Output", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
        L4 [label="Execution", fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
        { rank=same; L1; L2; L3; L4; }
    }
    Result -> L2 [style=invis];
}
```
label=fig:sample-and-test
caption=Codex (teal) samples k solutions (blue) for a problem (orange), unit tests (green) check each one, and the loop repeats for k samples.
::::
:::

* Assembling a Production Prompt

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// From internet resource GitHub Blog, "Inside GitHub: Working with the LLMs
// behind GitHub Copilot"
- @Problem@:
  - A raw autoregressive model only continues whatever text it is given
  - A coding assistant must decide _what text to give it_

- @Solution@:
  - A **production assistant** builds a prompt around the cursor position from
    several sources ("context"), not just the current file [@github2023inside]:
    - Code before and after the cursor
    - Recently viewed or edited files in the editor
    - File path and language, to bias style and imports
  - The "generation" a user sees is the model completing a prompt that was
    silently assembled on their behalf
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph ProductionPrompt {
    // orange: context source | teal: assembled prompt | blue: completion
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.15;
    ranksep=0.4;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    S1 [label="Code around\nthe cursor"];
    S2 [label="Recent files\nin the editor"];
    S3 [label="File path\nand language"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Prompt [label="Assembled\nprompt"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Completion [label="Completion\nat cursor"];

    { rank=same; S1; S2; S3; }
    S1 -> Prompt;
    S2 -> Prompt;
    S3 -> Prompt;
    Prompt -> Completion [label="  Model"];
}
```
label=fig:production-prompt
caption=Three context sources (orange) are assembled into one prompt (teal), which the model completes at the cursor (blue).
::::
:::

* Pattern Completion, Not Verification

- @Key idea@: code generation is repeated next-token prediction, trained on a
  mixture of scraped repositories, documentation, and synthetic code
- Two levers set completion quality:
  - _What_ the model was trained on
  - _What_ is placed in front of it as a prompt

- @Remark@:
  - The same mechanism generates:
    - Correct code it has never seen
    - Plausible code that is subtly wrong 
  - Both come from pattern continuation, not verified execution

## Context and Prompting Patterns

* Why Context Shapes Generation Quality

- @Motivation@:
  - The same model, given a different prompt, produces different code
    [@sclar2024quantifying]
  - **Prompting** is the main lever a user has over generation quality

- @Problem@: an under-specified prompt gives the model too many equally
  plausible continuations to choose from

- @Questions@:
  1. What goes into a prompt?
     - E.g., system prompts, few-shot examples, instructions
  2. How context window size limits and shapes multi-file generation?

* System Prompts, Instructions, and Completions

- @Completion@: the model is given a prefix (e.g., a function signature) and
  continues it directly
- @Instruction@: the model is given a natural-language request (e.g., _"add input
  validation to this function"_) and produces code as a response
- @System prompt@: a standing instruction set before the user's request, fixing role,
  constraints, and conventions for the whole session [@anthropic2026prompting]
// From internet resource Anthropic, "Prompt engineering overview"
  - @Example@: a system prompt stating _"follow this repo's existing style, never
    invent an API"_ changes every completion generated afterward, without repeating
    that constraint in each request

* Few-Shot Prompting for Code

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: **few-shot prompting** places one or more example
  `input -> output` pairs in the prompt before the actual task, so the model uses the
  pattern instead of guessing it from the instruction alone [@brown2020language]

// From paper Austin et al., "Program Synthesis with Large Language Models" (2021)
- @Fact@: providing a handful of example `(problem, solution)` pairs
  measurably improves program-synthesis accuracy over instruction-only
  prompting (across models, model sizes, etc)
  - E.g., pasting two existing test functions from a repo before asking for a
    third steers the model toward that repo's naming and assertion style

- @Intuition@: few-shot examples work because generation is pattern continuation:
  showing the pattern is often more effective than describing it
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph FewShotPrompt {
    // orange: prompt content | teal: model | blue: output
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    rankdir=LR;
    compound=true;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.15;
    ranksep=0.45;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    subgraph cluster_prompt {
        label="Prompt"; labelloc="t"; fontname="Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize=11.5;
        fontcolor="#3A4A5C"; style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#F7F9FB";
        color="#C7D0DA"; penwidth=1.0; margin=12;

        node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        E1 [label="Example 1\ninput -> output"];
        E2 [label="Example 2\ninput -> output"];
        E3 [label="New task\ninput -> ?"];
        { rank=same; E1; E2; E3; }
        E3 -> E2 -> E1 [style=invis];
    }

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Model [label="Model"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Out [label="Predicted\noutput"];

    E2 -> Model [ltail=cluster_prompt];
    Model -> Out;
}
```
label=fig:few-shot-prompt
caption=A few-shot prompt holds example pairs and a new task (orange), and the model (teal) continues the pattern to produce the predicted output (blue).
::::
:::

* Chain-of-Thought Prompting

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// From paper Wei et al., "Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in
// Large Language Models" (2022)
- @Definition@: **chain-of-thought prompting** asks the model to produce
  intermediate reasoning steps before the final answer, instead of jumping
  directly to it [@wei2022chain]

- @Example@:
  - Instead of _"write a function to merge two sorted lists"_
  - Prompt for _"first describe the algorithm in steps, then implement it"_

- @Intuition@:
  - Since generation is autoregressive, reasoning tokens written before the code
    become part of the context the code is conditioned on
  - The model reads its own plan while writing the implementation
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph DirectVsReasoning {
    // orange: prompt | teal: reasoning steps | blue: code
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.4;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.16,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Prompt [label="Prompt"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Plan [label="Reasoning\nsteps"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    CodeA [label="Code\n(direct)"];
    CodeB [label="Code\n(plan in context)"];

    Prompt -> CodeA [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", label="  Direct\l"];
    Prompt -> Plan [label="  Reason\l  first\l"];
    Plan -> CodeB;
    { rank=same; CodeA; Plan; }
}
```
label=fig:direct-vs-reasoning
caption=A direct prompt jumps to code, while a reasoning-first prompt puts plan tokens (teal) in the context before the code (blue) is written.
::::
:::

* Plan-Then-Implement Prompting

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
// From book Taulli, "AI-Assisted Programming" (2024)
- @Definition@: **plan-then-implement prompting** decomposes a coding
  request into an explicit planning stage and a separate implementation
  stage, rather than one prompt asking for a finished solution directly

- @Steps@:
  1. Ask for a plan: files to touch, functions to add, edge cases to handle
  2. Review or edit the plan
  3. Ask for implementation against the approved plan

- @Comparison@:
  - _Single-shot_: one prompt, one diff, no intermediate checkpoint
  - _Plan-then-implement_: a checkpoint between "what to build" and "how
    it's built", catching a wrong approach before code is written
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz[width=90%]
digraph PlanThenImplement {
    // orange: input | teal: model step | rose: human | blue: output
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.15;
    ranksep=0.16;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.05", height=0.32];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Req [label="Request"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Ask  [label="Ask for plan"];
    Impl [label="Implement\nagainst plan"];

    node [fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
    Rev [label="Review or\nedit plan"];

    node [fillcolor="#FFFFFF", color="#9AA9B8", fontcolor="#243B53"];
    Ok [label="Approved?"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Code [label="Code"];

    Req -> Ask -> Rev -> Ok;
    Ok -> Impl [label="  Yes"];
    Impl -> Code;
    Ok -> Ask [label="  No", style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", constraint=false];

    subgraph cluster_legend {
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#FBFBFD"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=10;
        node [fontsize=9, height=0.26, width=0.5, margin="0.08,0.04",
              penwidth=1.2];
        L1 [label="Input", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        L2 [label="Model", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
        L3 [label="Human", fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
        L4 [label="Output", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
        { rank=same; L1; L2; L3; L4; }
    }
    Code -> L2 [style=invis];
}
```
label=fig:plan-then-implement
caption=A request (orange) gets a plan from the model (teal), a human (rose) reviews it, and only an approved plan is implemented into code (blue).
::::
:::

* Prompting for Multi-File Tasks

- @Problem@: a single prompt-completion cycle produces one coherent continuation,
  but a real task often touches several files that must stay consistent with each
  other [@jimenez2024swebench]

- @Solution@: decompose the task into scoped, reviewable steps, each with its own
  prompt and its own check, rather than one prompt for the whole change

- @Example@: renaming a function used across a codebase decomposes into:
  - Locate call sites
  - Apply the rename
  - Update imports and tests
  so that each is a separate, independently checkable step

- @Key idea@: iterative refinement across scoped steps trades one large,
  hard-to-verify generation for several small, easy-to-verify ones

* Context Window: Definition

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a model's **context window** is the maximum number of tokens
  (prompt plus generated output combined) it can attend to for one request

- @Key idea@: everything the model "knows" about the current task (e.g., the
  file, the request, prior turns, retrieved snippets) must fit inside this
  budget, or it is simply not seen

- @Comparison@:
  - _Small context_: forces aggressive selection of what to include
  - _Large context_: allows more of the codebase in at once, but does not
    guarantee the model uses all of it equally well [@liu2024lost]
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph ContextBudget {
    // orange: tokens in the prompt | blue: generated tokens
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.12;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.16,0.08", height=0.4, width=2.2];
    edge [style=invis];

    subgraph cluster_window {
        label="Context window (fixed size)"; labelloc="t";
        fontname="Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize=11.5; fontcolor="#3A4A5C";
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=14;

        node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        Sys   [label="System prompt"];
        Files [label="File contents"];
        Snip  [label="Retrieved snippets"];
        Req   [label="Request"];

        node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
        Out [label="Generated output"];

        Sys -> Files -> Snip -> Req -> Out;
    }
}
```
label=fig:context-window
caption=Everything the model sees, the prompt parts (orange) and the generated output (blue), must fit in one fixed-size context window.
::::
:::

* Lost in the Middle

// From paper Liu et al., "Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long
// Contexts" (2023)
// TODO(gp): Make this horizontal?
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Fact@: retrieval accuracy from a long context is highest when the
  relevant information is near the _beginning_ or _end_ of the context, and
  degrades for information placed in the _middle_ [@liu2024lost]

- @Problem@: a large codebase does not fit in any context window, so a
  request touching several files competes for a limited token budget with
  the rest of the prompt
  - Including too little: the model has to guess the missing pieces
  - Including too much: relevant content risks landing in the lost middle

- @Remark@: retrieval strategies that select which files to include, rather
  than pasting all of them [@zhang2023repocoder]
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\normalsize}, x=0.7cm, y=0.7cm}
\definecolor{LostPink}{RGB}{244,166,166}
\draw[->, line width=0.4pt] (0,0) -- (9.4,0);
\draw[->, line width=0.4pt] (0,0) -- (0,6.2);
\fill[LostPink!35] (3.3,0) rectangle (6.1,5.8);
\node[align=center, text=red!60!black] at (4.7,4.9) {lost in the\\middle};
\draw[blue!70!black, line width=0.8pt, smooth]
  plot coordinates {(0.4,5.3) (1.4,4.0) (2.6,2.5) (4.7,1.5) (6.8,2.5) (8.0,4.0) (9.0,5.3)};
\node[below] at (0.6,0) {start};
\node[below] at (4.7,0) {middle};
\node[below] at (8.8,0) {end};
\node[below] at (4.7,-0.8) {Position of relevant text};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.6,3.1) {Retrieval accuracy};
```
label=fig:lost-in-the-middle
caption=Schematic: accuracy is highest near the start and end of the context.
::::
:::

* Anatomy of an Effective Code Prompt

// From internet resource Anthropic, "Prompt engineering overview"
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Components@ of a **well-formed code prompt**:
  - _System prompt_: role, conventions, constraints
  - _Context_: the specific files or snippets the task needs
  - _Instruction_: the concrete request, stated unambiguously
  - _Examples_ (optional): few-shot pairs showing the desired pattern

- @Key idea@:
  - Each component narrows the space of plausible continuations
  - A well-formed prompt is how a user supplies the information the model's
    training data alone cannot provide
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz[width=45%]
digraph PromptAnatomy {
  rankdir=TB;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10, penwidth=1.5];
  Sys [label="System prompt", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
  Ctx [label="Context", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
  Ex  [label="Examples", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
  Ins [label="Instruction", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
  Sys -> Ctx -> Ex -> Ins;
}
```
label=fig:prompt-anatomy
caption=A code prompt stacks a system prompt (light blue), context (orange), optional examples (teal), and an instruction (pink).
// TODO(gp): Explain this 
::::
:::

## Limits of Code Generation

* Why Code Generation Still Fails

- @Key idea@: prompting provides task-specific information, given a limited
  context window
  - E.g., system prompts, few-shot, chain-of-thought, plan-then-implement, scoped
    multi-file decomposition

- @Remark@: even a well-formed prompt does not guarantee correct code

- @Motivation@: an LLM that predicts plausible next tokens has no built-in
  notion of _"this API does not exist"_ or _"this codebase changed since
  training"_

- @Overview@: three structural limits
  1. Hallucinated APIs and outdated library versions
  2. Token and context budget limits on large codebases
  3. Degradation on long files and ambiguous specs

* Types of Hallucination: An Empirical View

// From paper Liu et al., "Exploring and Evaluating Hallucinations in
// LLM-Powered Code Generation" (2024)
- @Fact@: an empirical study across mainstream code-generation LLMs categorized
  hallucination types [@liu2024exploring]
  - Hallucinations happen across essentially every model tested, not just weaker
    or smaller ones

- @Comparison@: hallucinations range from:
  - _Syntactic_: code that does not even parse
  - _Semantic_: code that parses and runs, but does the wrong thing
  - _Knowledge-based_: code built on a wrong fact about a library or API
    - E.g., the hardest to catch during a review, since the code looks idiomatic
      and is internally consistent

// From paper Lee et al., "Hallucination by Code Generation LLMs: Taxonomy,
// Benchmarks, Mitigation, and Challenges" (2025)
- @Definition@: a **hallucination** in code generation is output that is
  plausible but factually or behaviorally wrong [@lee2025hallucination], e.g.,
  - _Invented APIs_: calls to functions or methods that never existed
  - _Incorrect library behavior_: a real API, but wrong assumed semantics
  - _Outdated usage_: an API that existed, but under a since-changed
    signature or a deprecated pattern

* Why Hallucination Happens

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Intuition@: the model was trained to continue plausible text, not to verify
  facts against a live environment
  - A rarely used but plausible-sounding function name can be more probable
    under the model's training distribution than the real, less common one

- @Key idea@: hallucination is not a bug that better training alone removes,
  it is a direct consequence of generation without execution or lookup
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph NextTokenOptions {
    // orange: prompt | teal: candidate names | blue: output | red edge: key path
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.45;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.16,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Prefix [label="Prompt prefix"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Invented [label="Plausible name\n(does not exist)"];
    Real     [label="Real name\n(less common)"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Chosen [label="Generated name"];

    Prefix -> Invented [label="  More\l  probable\l", penwidth=2.4,
                        color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B"];
    Prefix -> Real [label="  Less\l  probable\l"];
    Invented -> Chosen [penwidth=2.4, color="#C0455B"];
    { rank=same; Invented; Real; }
}
```
label=fig:next-token-options
caption=Given a prompt prefix (orange), a plausible but invented name (teal) can be more probable than the real, rarer one, so the model generates it (blue).
::::
:::

* Knowledge Conflicts

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// From paper Ashik et al., "When LLMs Lag Behind: Knowledge Conflicts from
// Evolving APIs in Code Generation" (2026)
- @Definition@: a **knowledge conflict** occurs when the model's memorized,
  pre-cutoff API pattern disagrees with the current version of the same library

- @Fact@: models default to the memorized, pre-cutoff pattern even when the
  prompt supplies the current API information directly [@ashik2026lag]
  - Up-to-date context does not automatically override a strongly memorized old
    pattern

- @Remark@: this is a stronger failure mode than plain hallucination, it
  persists even when the correct answer is already in the context
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph KnowledgeConflict {
    // orange: input | teal: model memory | blue: output
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    newrank=true;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.4;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    subgraph cluster_before {
        label="Before cutoff"; labelloc="t"; fontname="Helvetica-Bold";
        fontsize=11.5; fontcolor="#3A4A5C"; style="rounded,filled";
        fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA"; penwidth=1.0; margin=12;

        node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        Train [label="Training\ndata"];
        node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
        Memory [label="Memorized\nold API"];
        Train -> Memory;
    }

    subgraph cluster_after {
        label="After cutoff"; labelloc="t"; fontname="Helvetica-Bold";
        fontsize=11.5; fontcolor="#3A4A5C"; style="rounded,filled";
        fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA"; penwidth=1.0; margin=12;

        node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        Change [label="Library\nchanges API"];
        Prompt [label="Prompt shows\nnew API"];
        Change -> Prompt;
    }

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Out [label="Output uses\nold API"];

    { rank=same; Train; Change; }
    { rank=same; Memory; Prompt; }
    Train -> Change [style=invis];
    Memory -> Prompt [style=invis];
    Memory -> Out [penwidth=2.0];
    Prompt -> Out [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C",
                   label="  Not enough\l  to override\l"];
}
```
label=fig:knowledge-conflict
caption=Training data (orange) leaves a memorized old API (teal), and a prompt showing the new API (orange) does not override it, so the output (blue) uses the old API.
::::
:::

* Execution-Based Verification of Hallucination

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
// From paper Tian et al., "CodeHalu: Investigating Code Hallucinations in
// LLMs via Execution-based Verification" (2024)
- @Definition@: an **execution-based benchmark** detects hallucination by
  running generated code against test inputs and checking outputs, rather
  than only inspecting the code's text [@tian2024codehalu]

- @Remark@: this motivates treating test execution, not code inspection, as the
  ground truth for judging correctness
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph RunVsRead {
    // blue: code and verdict | green: execution | teal: comparison | gray: weak check
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.35;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Code   [label="Generated code"];
    Result [label="Pass / fail"];

    node [fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Run [label="Run on\ntest inputs"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Compare [label="Compare with\nexpected output"];

    node [style="rounded,filled,dashed", fillcolor="#F7F9FB", color="#9AA9B8",
          fontcolor="#3A4A5C"];
    Read  [label="Read code\ntext only"];
    Looks [label="Looks correct\n(not ground truth)"];

    Code -> Run -> Compare -> Result;
    Code -> Read [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    Read -> Looks [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    { rank=same; Run; Read; }
    { rank=same; Compare; Looks; }
}
```
label=fig:run-vs-read
caption=Generated code (blue) is run on test inputs (green) and compared with the expected output (teal), while reading the code text (dashed gray) is not ground truth.
::::
:::

* Context Budget on Large Codebases

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Problem@: even the largest available context window is finite, while a
  real codebase can be orders of magnitude larger

- @Implication@: a request touching a large codebase always operates on a
  _subset_ of the code, selected by some retrieval or truncation strategy

- @Example@: asking an assistant to "make this change consistent across the
  whole codebase" in one prompt silently limits "whole codebase" to whatever
  fit in the context window, not the literal whole codebase
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
```graphviz
digraph CodebaseSubset {
    // gray dashed: unseen files | orange: selected | blue: window | teal: model
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.2;
    ranksep=0.4;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.14,0.08", height=0.4];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    subgraph cluster_codebase {
        label="Codebase (far larger than the window)"; labelloc="t";
        fontname="Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize=11.5; fontcolor="#3A4A5C";
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=14;

        node [style="rounded,filled,dashed", fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
              color="#9AA9B8", fontcolor="#3A4A5C", fontsize=10,
              margin="0.1,0.05", height=0.32];
        F1 [label="auth.py"];
        F2 [label="db.py"];
        F3 [label="cli.py"];
        F4 [label="config.py"];
        F8 [label="utils.py"];

        node [style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F",
              fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        F5 [label="api.py"];
        F6 [label="models.py"];
        F7 [label="tests.py"];

        { rank=same; F1; F2; F3; F4; }
        { rank=same; F5; F6; F7; F8; }
        F1 -> F5 [style=invis];
        F2 -> F6 [style=invis];
        F3 -> F7 [style=invis];
        F4 -> F8 [style=invis];
    }

    node [fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.16,0.08", height=0.4];
    Window [label="Context window\n(selected files only)"];

    node [fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Model [label="Model"];

    F5 -> Window;
    F6 -> Window;
    F7 -> Window;
    Window -> Model;
}
```
label=fig:codebase-subset
caption=Retrieval selects a few files (orange) from the codebase into the context window (blue) for the model (teal), and the unselected dashed files are never seen.
::::
:::

* When Generation Degrades: Long Files

- @Problem@: as a single file grows, more of it competes for the same context
  budget, and more of it risks landing in the lost middle context window (or
  outside)

- @Comparison@:
  - _Short, focused file_: most content stays near the start or end of
    context, where retrieval is most reliable
  - _Long, monolithic file_: relevant constraints are more likely to be
    buried in the middle

* When Generation Degrades: Ambiguous Specs

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Problem@: an **ambiguous natural-language spec** has multiple plausible
  continuations, and the model picks one without asking for clarification unless
  explicitly prompted to, e.g.,
  - _"add caching to this function"_ is ambiguous over cache key, eviction
    policy, thread-safety, ...
  - The model picks _a reasonable default_, not necessarily _the_ one the
    codebase needs

- @Remark@: writing specs that eliminate this ambiguity replaces the model's
  freedom to guess with a deliberate, reviewable design choice instead
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
```graphviz
digraph AmbiguousSpec {
    // orange: request | teal dashed: model default | blue: spec decision
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.1";
    rankdir=LR;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.12;
    ranksep=0.4;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06", height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7];

    node [fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Root [label="Add caching\nto this function"];

    node [fillcolor="#FFFFFF", color="#9AA9B8", fontcolor="#243B53"];
    Key    [label="Cache key?"];
    Evict  [label="Eviction\npolicy?"];
    Thread [label="Thread\nsafety?"];

    node [style="rounded,filled,dashed", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890",
          fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    D1 [label="Model picks\na default"];
    D2 [label="Model picks\na default"];
    D3 [label="Model picks\na default"];

    node [style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE",
          fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    S1 [label="Spec decides"];
    S2 [label="Spec decides"];
    S3 [label="Spec decides"];

    Root -> Key;
    Root -> Evict;
    Root -> Thread;
    Key    -> D1 [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    Key    -> S1;
    Evict  -> D2 [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    Evict  -> S2;
    Thread -> D3 [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    Thread -> S3;
}
```
label=fig:ambiguous-spec
caption=An ambiguous request (orange) leaves several choices open, and each is either filled by a model default (dashed teal) or decided in the spec (blue).
::::
:::

* Survey View: Where the Field Stands

// From paper Huynh et al., "Large Language Models for Code Generation: A
// Comprehensive Survey" (2025)
- @Fact@: a comprehensive survey of code-generation LLMs finds that
  - Architecture and training-data improvements have steadily raised benchmark
    scores
  - Hallucination and context limits remain open challenges across model families
  - Better models shift where the limits show up (e.g., longer reliable context,
    fewer invented APIs)

* One Root Cause for Every Limit

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Limit", "Root cause"),
  rows: (
    ("Hallucinated APIs", "Pattern completion without lookup"),
    ("Stale API knowledge", "Training-data cutoff vs. current library"),
    ("Context budget limits", "Finite context vs. large codebases"),
    ("Ambiguous specs", "Multiple plausible continuations"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 2fr),
)
```

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Key idea@: every limit in this lesson traces back to the same root
  - _"An autoregressive model continues plausible patterns, it does not verify
    facts or execute code on its own"_
  - LLM needs grounding in codebase, updated documentation, or execution

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```mermaid
mindmap
  root((**LLM Code Generation**))
    (**Generation**)
      Autoregressive prediction
      Training data mix
    (**Prompting**)
      System prompts
      Few-shot / chain-of-thought
      Context window
    (**Limits**)
      Hallucinated APIs
      Knowledge conflicts
      Context budget
      Ambiguous specs
```
::::
:::

* Open Questions

- How much of a codebase should a prompt include before more context starts
  hurting, rather than helping, generation quality? [@shi2023large]
- Can a model be made to signal its own uncertainty about an API call, instead of
  generating a confident but wrong one?
- Where is the boundary between an ambiguous spec the model should ask about and
  one it is reasonable to resolve with a default?

